<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ælfgar, Earl of Mercia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1062</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Ælfgar of Mercia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ælfgar was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> nobleman, son of Earl Leofric and Lady Godiva, who became Earl of East Anglia and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-mercia">Earl of Mercia</a>. Outlawed in 1055, he allied with the Welsh king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-llywelyn">Gruffudd ap Llywelyn</a>, who married his daughter, and together they sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a>. He was restored to his earldom, outlawed again in 1058 and again returned with Welsh and Norse help, and died around 1062.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aelfgar-earl-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Ælfgar, Earl of Mercia" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guthlac_Roll_-_The_benefactors.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
